# Impact Registry — shared code awareness (M1i)

The gate stops the AI from *writing*; M1i stops it from writing **blind**.
When a proposal targets a registered shared module, it must first prove —
via LSP — what that change affects.

## Why

- Duplicated logic grows when the model re-implements what a central module
  already provides. The registry names the canonical implementations so
  reuse is visible, not accidental.
- "I changed the shared thing and something else broke" happens when the
  change was proposed without knowing its consumers. The impact list forces
  that knowledge into every proposal touching shared code.
- Flip-flop breakage is a review-time problem: with `impact` recorded on the
  proposal, the nvim side shows *what the change touches* before you apply.

## The registry file (`.opencode/manage.registry.json`)

Fail-open: **no registry file = no impact requirement.** Projects opt in.

```json
{
  "shared": [
    {
      "path": "internal/store/store.go",
      "symbols": ["Save", "Load"],
      "fragile": true,
      "constraints": "Do not change function signatures; additive changes only"
    }
  ],
  "canonical": [
    {
      "concept": "time formatting",
      "path": "internal/timefmt/format.go",
      "symbols": ["FormatDuration"]
    }
  ]
}
```

- `shared`: central modules. A proposal whose `path` equals a shared path —
  or lives inside one — **must** carry `impact`.
- `fragile`: marks the entry as high-risk; the summary shows `[FRAGILE]` so
  the model treats it with care.
- `constraints`: rules the model sees before proposing (no signature
  changes, additive only, etc.).
- `canonical`: "for this concept, reuse this implementation" — the dedup
  map. Model-facing only; no structural gate.

## The impact requirement

`emit_proposal` on a shared path with a missing/empty/invalid `impact` throws
a **requirements error** (⚠️, not ❌):

- missing → "run lsp_find_references on the symbols you are changing"
- unparseable / wrong shape → "must be a JSON array of {path, symbol, relation}"
- path not on disk → "impact must come from real LSP results, not invention"

This is the ONE deliberate exception to DENIAL IS TERMINAL: the model must
retry with evidence, not give up. The proposal is not recorded until `impact`
is valid.

## What impact looks like on a proposal

```json
{
  "ts": 1780000000000,
  "path": "internal/store/store.go",
  "impact": [
    { "path": "internal/game/game.go", "symbol": "Save", "relation": "calls" },
    { "path": "internal/score/score.go", "symbol": "Load", "relation": "reads" }
  ],
  "reuses": [
    { "path": "internal/timefmt/format.go", "symbol": "FormatDuration" }
  ]
}
```

- `impact`: consumers of the changed symbols — gathered via LSP, verified to
  exist on disk.
- `reuses`: canonical implementations the change reuses — proof of no
  duplication.

## Cost

- Gate: one extra tiny sync read per tool call (~0.1ms) — imperceptible.
- Only proposals touching registered shared paths pay the LSP cost (1-3
  extra calls, seconds) — which is the verification you asked for.
- No registry file = current behavior, unchanged.

